  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Rafael Devers hit two and the bullpen never flinched. They took what they needed and held.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed after one inning, then answered with four runs in the first two. Devers drove in two of them with his homer in the second. The offense managed just five hits and struck out eight times, yet the moment it mattered most. Runners in scoring position early. They converted one run from six chances. That's thin production doing heavy work. Adrian Houser took over after Brubaker's quick exit and threw five scoreless innings with four strikeouts. The back end held: Winn, Hentges, Smith combining for two and a third clean innings. It was not dominant. It was not comfortable. It was exactly what a fragile team needs. A win that doesn't expose anything new. They came back from behind, which matters. They didn't squander the lead, which matters more. This is what one-run wins look like when they stack: narrow margins, lucky conversions, relief arms that don't blink. The problem is the math. Three straight one-run wins is not a formula. It's running out the clock.",
    "what_to_watch": "Detroit starts Jackson Jobe tonight. A young arm with a 3.87 ERA in a series the Giants can't afford to drop. Landen Roupp gets the ball again on short rest.",
